        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> At first glance, this doesn't look like an intended solution because it will set terminal background to #000000 (i.e. pure black) which might contradict the whole \"transparent background\" idea.\r\n\r\nto my knowlage, most \"transparent\" themes set the background to \"none\", and the terminal will print its default background. because of this, if a transparent theme is used with a non-transparent terminal, the background will just be the color of the terminal. You're right that setting it to black will set the color of the terminal to black, but the transparency will still be in effect.\r\n I've tested this with kitty, alacritty( non-transparent background), and my main terminal emulator, ghostty. The only difference is that in kitty, it seems all backgrounds, even for the non-transparent themes, have the opacity effect applied onto them.\r\n \r\n in my last commit i moved the reset function above sync and call that when there is no background, which should fix the pure black background\r\n \r\n first result from setting the theme to catpuccin, then to the transparent theme \r\n<img width=\"483\" height=\"515\" alt=\"2025-08-19-042735_hyprshot\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/42e7bb38-be18-41a7-9dda-b25447f1f154\" />\r\nsame actions with the first \"fix\" \r\n<img width=\"429\" height=\"430\" alt=\"2025-08-19-042719_hyprshot\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7887c096-9a50-40bc-adb1-114cc610f47d\" />\r\nsame actions with my last commit\r\n<img width=\"637\" height=\"630\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4bec2f35-36cd-4ce0-9bd1-ed51988a0d13\" />\r\n",
